# DSA-301 Mid-Term Test — Batch 2 (15 challenges)

Author-ready markdown for `automator.py`. Fifteen problems for a DSA-301 Mid-Term Test contest. Each challenge carries 10 sample I/O pairs; run `tools/hr_mark_samples.py --md dsa301_midterm_test_v2.md --apply` to flag the first two as samples. **Ensure ONLY C++14 and Java 8 are enabled on each challenge — unselect every other language (Python 3, etc.)** before publishing. Tree problems (Q4, Q5, Q8, Q9, Q10) ship with Head Code / Solution Template / Tail Code boilerplate in both C++14 and Java 8 — paste them into the HackerRank admin **Head Code / Body / Tail Code** slots after upload (the automator does not parse boilerplate blocks).

Section weights: Section A (Q1–Q5) — 10 marks each. Section B (Q6–Q15) — 20 marks each.

Difficulty per challenge (set in the HackerRank admin UI after upload; the automator does not set this field):

| # | Title | Section | Difficulty |
|---|-------|---------|-----------|
| Q1 | Midterm: Move Zeroes | A | Easy |
| Q2 | Midterm: Valid Palindrome | A | Easy |
| Q3 | Midterm: Search Insert Position | A | Easy |
| Q4 | Midterm: Binary Tree Preorder Traversal | A | Easy |
| Q5 | Midterm: Invert Binary Tree | A | Easy |
| Q6 | Midterm: Product of Array Except Self | B | Medium |
| Q7 | Midterm: Asteroid Collision | B | Medium |
| Q8 | Midterm: Zigzag Level Order Traversal of a Binary Tree | B | Medium |
| Q9 | Midterm: Validate Binary Search Tree v2 | B | Medium |
| Q10 | Midterm: Kth Smallest Element in a BST | B | Medium |
| Q11 | Midterm: First and Last Position in Sorted Array | B | Medium |
| Q12 | Midterm: Top K Frequent Elements | B | Medium |
| Q13 | Midterm: Room Allocation | B | Medium |
| Q14 | Midterm: Subarray Sum Equals K | B | Medium |
| Q15 | Midterm: Minimum Window Substring | B | Medium |

**Constraint philosophy.** Every constraint is sized so that a naive brute-force solution will time out on the full test set and only an intended-complexity solution passes. The sample I/O remains small for human inspection; HackerRank's hidden test cases should include at least one near-maximum input per problem to force the asymptotic cut-off.

**Tree input convention (Q4, Q5, Q8, Q9, Q10)**: a binary tree is encoded on one line as a space-separated level-order sequence of tokens, where each token is either an integer value or the literal `null` for an absent child. The first token is the root. Reading proceeds level-by-level: for each non-null node in order, the next two tokens are its left and right children. The provided Head Code parses this format and hands the user a `TreeNode* root` (C++) / `TreeNode root` (Java).

